# The Swarm, Counted

### Paper I was the root. Paper II was the perimeter. This is the network itself — counted.

**Anonymous security research — November 2026**
**Companion to "One Root: The Session Identity Architecture" and "The Perimeter." This paper covers disjoint ground: neither the cryptography of an account nor the external systems a client contacts, but the service-node network that carries everything — measured, from the network's own records, at a single snapshot: 2026-11-04, 04:23 UTC. Signed by the same key.**

- ---

## Abstract

Session's decentralization claim rests on a number: over a thousand independent
service nodes, operated by the community, among whom message custody is distributed
by swarm. This paper counts what the number counts. The node list was obtained twice,
from two independent sources — the protocol's own daemon RPC
(`get_service_nodes`, via the official public endpoint) and the project's public
block explorer — and the two lists are **identical**: 1,163 node public keys, zero
discrepancy in either direction. Every figure below is computed from that snapshot
with a published script and a bulk IP-to-ASN dataset; the snapshot hashes are listed
at the end.

The 1,163 nodes resolve to **653 IP addresses, 124 operators, and 70 hosting
providers**. Seventy-two multi-node operators run 95.5% of the network. Five hosting
companies hold 67.8% of all nodes; legal process, logging, or compromise directed at
**four providers reaches half the network's message custody**, and at eight
providers, three-quarters of it. For 24 of the 187 storage swarms — one in eight —
a single provider holds at least half the nodes a user's messages are entrusted to.
And every one of the 1,163 nodes is staked at exactly the protocol minimum, to the
coin: a pattern with no exceptions, in either of two snapshots taken two days apart.

## 1. The count, and what it counts

A service node is an entry in a list. The list is public, the count is real, and
this paper does not dispute it: at the snapshot, 1,163 active, funded nodes.

Independence is a different quantity, and it is measured on different axes:

| Axis | Count |
|---|---|
| Nodes | 1,163 |
| Unique public IP addresses | 653 |
| Unique /24 networks | 522 |
| Unique hosting providers (AS organisations) | 70 |
| Unique operator addresses | 124 |

A node count is an **upper bound** on independent operators, not a count of them —
a distinction the project's own tooling concedes, since it ships multi-node
packaging (`session-multi-sn`) for running many nodes from one operator on one
machine, whose README warns that co-hosting concentrates failure. What the snapshot
adds is the size of the gap: on the operator axis, the advertised number overstates
independence by a factor of roughly **nine**. The network is not 1,163 parties. It
is, at most, 124 — before asking how many of those 124 are the same hands under
different addresses, a question this paper deliberately does not answer from
address data alone (§8).

## 2. One address, fifty-five nodes

**624 of the 1,163 nodes — 53.7% — share an IP address with at least one other
node.** 114 addresses host more than one node. The busiest:

| Nodes | Share | Address |
|---|---|---|
| 55 | 4.73% | 95.217.37.13 |
| 30 | 2.58% | 95.216.33.113 |
| 29 | 2.49% | 208.73.207.54 |
| 23 | 1.98% | 172.93.103.156 |
| 19 | 1.63% | 212.105.90.36 |
| 19 | 1.63% | 37.27.236.229 |
| 16 | 1.38% | 185.150.191.51 |

One IP address is the network location of **4.7% of the entire network**. Co-location
at this scale is not an abuse of the design; it *is* the design — the multi-node
packaging exists to make one well-run server present as many nodes. But a swarm slot
filled by a co-hosted node is not an independent copy of a user's messages in any
sense that survives a subpoena, a seizure, a provider outage, or a compromise of the
host: all the copies on that address share one fate. The protocol counts them
separately. Physics does not.

## 3. Five landlords

Resolve the addresses to their hosting providers and the "community network" takes
its physical shape — rented virtual servers at a short list of budget hosters:

| Provider | Nodes | Share |
|---|---|---|
| Hetzner | 266 | 22.87% |
| Contabo | 157 | 13.50% |
| OVH | 142 | 12.21% |
| NodeHub | 116 | 9.97% |
| ReliableSite | 107 | 9.20% |
| Akamai / Linode | 36 | 3.10% |
| DigitalOcean | 29 | 2.49% |
| InterServer | 29 | 2.49% |
| ColoCrossing / HostPapa | 27 | 2.32% |
| netcup | 22 | 1.89% |

**The top five providers hold 67.75% of all nodes. The top ten hold 80.04%.** The
remaining sixty providers share the last fifth. There is nothing clandestine here —
the addresses are public by protocol design — but the picture deserves to be stated
flatly: the decentralized network's message custody physically resides, two-thirds
of it, in the racks of five companies, under five terms-of-service agreements, in a
handful of jurisdictions, reachable by five legal-process desks.

## 4. The workforce is registered

Nodes are run by operators, identified in the protocol by their staking address.
There are **124** of them. Their distribution:

| Nodes | Share | Operator |
|---|---|---|
| 139 | 11.95% | 0xD982…6AE6 |
| 61 | 5.25% | 0x226F…9F73 |
| 55 | 4.73% | 0x3aC8…708f |
| 46 | 3.96% | 0x9a28…E12F |
| 45 | 3.87% | 0x87A8…b9dD |
| 42 | 3.61% | 0x0D61…970f |
| 39 | 3.35% | 0xACf7…3919 |
| 32 | 2.75% | 0xC587…F1b1 |
| 29 | 2.49% | 0x5C8d…f360 |
| 29 | 2.49% | 0x9bA3…f475 |

**72 operators run more than one node; together they run 1,111 of the 1,163 —
95.5%.** The largest single operator holds 139 nodes: roughly one node in eight,
network-wide, answers to one wallet.

Nor are these operators anonymous to the foundation. Registration runs through the
foundation's staking backend, whose schema this audit read in the server
repositories (Paper I's audit, Pass 20): a **registrations staging table joins each
operator's Arbitrum wallet address to their node's BLS and Ed25519 public keys,
with both signatures and a timestamp** — the wallet-to-node identity binding,
recorded server-side on foundation infrastructure before it ever lands on-chain.
Node operation is public by design; the point is narrower and harder: the operator
layer of this network is a *registered workforce*, enumerated in a foundation-held
table, paid through a foundation-run rewards pipeline. Registered, paid, and — as
§7 makes concrete — few.

One further observation, labelled as what it is: contributor addresses (the wallets
that fund stakes) overlap heavily — 237 unique contributors across the network, one
appearing on 157 nodes, 42 appearing on ten or more. Shared funding *suggests*
common operation without proving it, and no operator figure in this paper is built
on contributor data; the operator figures above use `operator_address` only. The
suggestion is recorded because a reader reproducing this work will see it, and it
points the same direction as everything else in the snapshot.

## 5. The draw

Users do not use "the network." Each account is assigned, by a public function of
its Session ID (Paper I), to one **swarm** — a small set of nodes that stores its
messages. The snapshot holds **187 swarms of 5 to 7 nodes** (mean 6.22), averaging
4.8 distinct providers and 5.74 distinct operators each. That average is the good
news, and it is recorded. The distribution has a tail:

- - **24 swarms — 12.8% — have a single provider holding at least 50% of their
  nodes.** The five worst are 5-node swarms in which **4 of 5 nodes are Hetzner**:
  one company's logs cover 80% of those users' message custody.
- - **5 swarms (2.7%) have a single operator holding at least 50%.**
- - **2 swarms (1.1%) have a single /24 holding at least 50%.**
- - No swarm is 100% single-provider. Stated plainly, because it is true, and
  because it changes nothing about the 24.

Translate the statistic into the user's position: roughly **one account in eight**
drew a swarm in which one hosting company can observe — or be made to produce —
the majority of that account's message traffic custody: which nodes hold their
ciphertexts, in what volume, on what timing, for the full retention window. And
because the assignment is computable by anyone from the account's public ID, an
interested party does not need to find a target's swarm by investigation. They
compute it, then look up who runs it — in a list where the answer, one time in
eight, is mostly one landlord.

## 6. Exactly the minimum

Every service node must stake 25,000 SESH. At the snapshot, total stake is
**29,075,000 SESH — which is 25,000 × 1,163, exactly**. All 1,163 nodes sit at the
requirement; zero sit above it; zero below. The earlier crawl, two days prior,
found the identical pattern at 1,162 nodes (29,050,000 SESH).

Read carefully, with the inference labelled: the figures prove uniformity, not
motive. But uniformity at a floor, with no exceptions across 1,163 positions, is
the fingerprint of cost-minimised fleet operation — stake treated as the rent on a
revenue position, sized to the minimum the protocol accepts. A network of 1,163
independent believers would show a distribution. A fleet shows a constant. The
snapshot shows a constant.

## 7. The pressure arithmetic

Combine the layers into the only question the decentralization claim ultimately
answers — *how many doors does pressure have to knock on?* Greedy set cover over
the snapshot:

| Custody reached | Hosting providers | Operators |
|---|---|---|
| 25% of nodes | **2** | 4 |
| 50% of nodes | **4** | 13 |
| 75% of nodes | **8** | 28 |

Compel, log, purchase, or compromise **two companies** and a quarter of the
network's message custody is inside the room. **Four companies: half. Eight:
three-quarters.** By operator the numbers are nearly as small — four operators for
the first quarter of the network. And for a *targeted* account, §5 applies instead:
the relevant number is not a share of the network but the majority holder of one
swarm, and one time in eight that holder is a single provider.

This is what "the node layer falls under pressure" means in numbers. Not that the
network has been pressured — this paper measures structure, not events — but that
its layout prices pressure in single digits.

## 8. What this paper does not claim

- - An IP address is not necessarily one machine, and co-location proves shared fate
  at the address level, not single ownership. The operator figures rest on
  `operator_address`, the protocol's own identity for who runs a node.
- - Contributor overlap is suggestive only and is used for no claim.
- - This is one snapshot (2026-11-04 04:23 UTC). The node set churns slowly — the
  count moved by one node in two days — but any reader reproducing this work later
  will get slightly different figures, and should: the method, not the moment, is
  the finding.
- - The explorer publishes the same list this paper counts. Nothing here was hidden;
  the gap is between the count's rhetorical use — *a thousand independent nodes* —
  and the independence the count actually contains.
- - Custody concentration is not decryption. Message content remains sealed exactly
  as Paper I bounded it; this paper is about who holds the ciphertexts, in what
  concentration, under whose roof — which is the ground on which every other
  property of the system ultimately stands.
- - A concentrated network still functions. The claim tested here is not uptime; it
  is the independence story the count is used to tell.

## 9. What a fix requires

The protocol cannot mint independent operators, but it can stop pretending that
co-hosted, co-provided nodes are independent for custody purposes: **swarm
assignment with diversity caps** — no more than a set fraction of a swarm's nodes
from one provider, one operator, or one /24 — is an engineering change, not a
research problem. Publishing the live provider/operator distribution alongside the
node count would let the claim and the measurement travel together. And the
staking backend's registration table, like every linkage database in Papers I and
II, should be treated as what it is: part of the system's real trust surface,
disclosed as such.

None of this requires new mathematics either. It requires counting the network the
way its adversaries will: not by its entries, but by its hands.

